WEBVTT

00:00:00.137 --> 00:00:06.222
Let's summarize Module 2. A strong relational table has a clear subject and a clear row grain.

00:00:06.222 --> 00:00:08.452
Keys solve different problems. Primary and surrogate keys provide stable row identity,

00:00:08.452 --> 00:00:11.997
natural and alternate keys protect business identifiers, composite keys identify combinations,

00:00:11.997 --> 00:00:15.412
and foreign keys connect related tables.

00:00:15.412 --> 00:00:20.567
Cardinality tells us where foreign keys belong and when a junction table is needed.

00:00:20.567 --> 00:00:22.932
Constraints turn important business rules into database-enforced guardrails.

00:00:22.932 --> 00:00:28.542
Normalization reduces repeated facts and update, insertion, and deletion anomalies.

00:00:28.542 --> 00:00:32.162
Finally, good operational design creates a more trustworthy source for analytical models.

00:00:32.162 --> 00:00:35.832
The coding habit to carry forward is simple: run a correct example, change one part, complete one rule,

00:00:35.832 --> 00:00:40.012
and then write a small version yourself.

00:00:40.012 --> 00:00:42.562
Module 3 builds on this foundation with querying and controlled data changes.

00:00:42.562 --> 00:00:45.367
Do not treat this as a list to memorize.

00:00:45.367 --> 00:00:48.607
Try to explain each idea using one example from a project you understand.

00:00:48.607 --> 00:00:53.282
If you can describe when you would use the concept, what assumption it makes,

00:00:53.282 --> 00:00:56.400
and how you would validate the result, you are ready to apply it independently.
